Wait, there's a subtle bug in my new attribute matcher: `[attr="value"]` (no operator) — regex: `^([a-zA-Z-]+)\s*(?:([\^\$\*])=(["']?)(.*)\3)?$` — for `[id="x"]`: am[1]="id", am[2]=undefined (no operator since `=` isn't preceded by ^$*... wait, the regex group 2 is `([\^\$\*])` followed by `=`. For `id="x"`, after `id` comes `=` directly — group2 can't match `=` (it only matches ^$*), so the optional group fails → am[2] undefined → the whole `(?:...)?` matches empty → then `$` requires end — but there's still `="x"` left → NO MATCH → throws "unsupported". Bug!

The original regex had `(?:=(["']?)(.*)\2)?` — plain `=`. I need BOTH: exact `=` and operator variants. Better regex:
`^([a-zA-Z-]+)\s*(?:([\^\$\*])=|(=))(["']?)(.*?)\4$` — getting messy. Cleaner approach: split on first `=`:

```js
const inner = raw.slice(1, -1);
const eq = inner.indexOf("=");
if (eq === -1) { presence check with name=inner.trim(); }
else {
  const name = inner.slice(0, eq).replace(/[\^\$\*]$/, "");
  const op = inner[eq-1] && /[\^\$\*]/.test(inner[eq-1]) ? inner[eq-1] : "=";
  ...
}
```
Hmm — name "id$" → strip trailing operator char → op = that char. Let me rewrite the loop cleanly.

